    <briefdescription>
<para><ref refid="namespacesf_1_1Mouse" kindref="compound">Mouse</ref> move raw event subtype. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Raw mouse input data comes unprocessed from the operating system hence &quot;raw&quot;. While the <ref refid="structsf_1_1Event_1_1MouseMoved" kindref="compound">MouseMoved</ref> position value is dependent on the screen resolution, raw data is not. If the physical mouse is moved too little to cause the screen cursor to move at least a single pixel, no <ref refid="structsf_1_1Event_1_1MouseMoved" kindref="compound">MouseMoved</ref> event will be generated. In contrast, any movement information generated by the mouse independent of its sensor resolution will always generate a <computeroutput><ref refid="structsf_1_1Event_1_1MouseMovedRaw" kindref="compound">MouseMovedRaw</ref></computeroutput> event.</para>
<para>In addition to screen resolution independence, raw mouse data also does not have mouse acceleration or smoothing applied to it as <ref refid="structsf_1_1Event_1_1MouseMoved" kindref="compound">MouseMoved</ref> does.</para>
<para>Raw mouse movement data is intended for controlling non-cursor movement, e.g. controlling the camera orientation in a first person view, whereas <ref refid="structsf_1_1Event_1_1MouseMoved" kindref="compound">MouseMoved</ref> is intended primarily for controlling things related to the screen cursor hence the additional processing applied to it.</para>
<para>Currently, raw mouse input events will only be generated on Windows and Linux. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
